      "notes": "Verdict is well-structured and passes all checks. The direction/magnitude combination (negligible/minor) is internally consistent \u2014 the rationale correctly applies the mechanism-plausibility-is-not-effect rule and the magnitude floor test. All claims are supported by their cited quotes. Tier assignments are appropriate: E1 (stated \u2014 from government sources describing the policy's own intent), E11/E22 (measurable \u2014 parliamentary committee findings and IFS analysis), E23/E17 (projected \u2014 analytical assessments). No lever leakage: the verdict judges effect on living standards/productivity/opportunity (O13), not the policy as a lever. No party-blind issues: the rationale attributes no motive and is framed around modelled effects. No false balance: the direction 'negligible' is not a mixed verdict requiring claims on both sides. Minor observation: E1 is tagged 'stated' but is sourced from third-party outlets rather than the policy text itself \u2014 this is a minor tier ambiguity but not blocking, as the claim it supports is factual and low-stakes. The \u00a32.1bn total cost arithmetic (105 \u00d7 \u00a320m) is correct. The verdict's honest acknowledgement of absence of evidence for population-scale effect is methodologically sound."
